"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms=globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms||[]).push([[7484],{8970(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"Proofs","permalink":"/concepts/tags/proofs","allTagsPath":"/concepts/tags","count":8,"items":[{"id":"backtracking/proofs","title":"Proofs: Backtracking","description":"Backtracking is an exact method: it promises to find every solution, or the best one. That promise depends on the pruning being safe, that is, never discarding a branch that contains what we are looking for. This page proves when that is the case.","permalink":"/concepts/backtracking/proofs"},{"id":"branch-and-bound/proofs","title":"Proofs: Branch and Bound","description":"Branch and bound finds the optimal solution only if its bounds and heuristics are valid. This page proves the facts used in the lessons: why the bounds of the examples are valid, why best-first branch and bound and A\\\\* stop at the optimum, and why the puzzle heuristics and the parity condition work. Safe pruning with a bound is proved in the backtracking chapter.","permalink":"/concepts/branch-and-bound/proofs"},{"id":"divide-and-conquer/proofs","title":"Proofs: Divide and Conquer","description":"This page proves the formulas used to analyze divide and conquer algorithms and the correctness and cost of several of them. The general techniques (induction, recursion trees, geometric series) are in the Mathematical Background appendix.","permalink":"/concepts/divide-and-conquer/proofs"},{"id":"dynamic-programming/proofs","title":"Proofs: Dynamic Programming","description":"A dynamic programming algorithm is correct if its recurrence is correct: the optimal value of each subproblem must be obtained from the optimal values of smaller subproblems. This page proves the recurrences used in the lessons.","permalink":"/concepts/dynamic-programming/proofs"},{"id":"greedy-algorithms/proofs","title":"Proofs: Greedy Algorithms","description":"A greedy algorithm is only useful if we can prove that its choices lead to an optimal solution. This page proves the optimality of the greedy algorithms that the lessons call optimal, mostly with exchange arguments: take any optimal solution and show that it can be changed, step by step, into the greedy one without getting worse.","permalink":"/concepts/greedy-algorithms/proofs"},{"id":"parallel-algorithms/proofs","title":"Proofs: Parallel Algorithms","description":"This page derives the formulas of the performance lesson and proves that the Fork/Join scheme computes the same result as the sequential algorithm. Times are measured relative to the sequential time, which we take as 1 unless stated otherwise.","permalink":"/concepts/parallel-algorithms/proofs"},{"id":"principles-of-algorithmics/proofs","title":"Proofs: Principles of Algorithmics","description":"This page collects the proofs behind the claims of the chapter. The lessons state the results and use them; here they are justified. Each section is self-contained, and the lessons link to the section they need. The proof techniques (induction and loop invariants) are explained in Induction, Correctness and Recurrences.","permalink":"/concepts/principles-of-algorithmics/proofs"},{"id":"sorting/proofs","title":"Proofs: Sorting","description":"This page proves the claims made in the sorting lessons: that each algorithm sorts correctly, the costs given in the lessons, and the limits that no comparison-based algorithm can break. The proofs use loop invariants and induction, explained in Induction, Correctness and Recurrences. Arrays are indexed from 0 and have $n$ elements.","permalink":"/concepts/sorting/proofs"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);